    "explanation": "Both of you: this is a route-deviation dispute over TRP-05003, and the rider's 4.50 SGD claim is not upheld. The 22.60 SGD metered fare stands, and no amount is refunded or paid.\n\nDeviation (POL-RD-22). Both sides agree on the measure, and so do I: the platform's recorded distances are 11.4 km actual minus 10.0 km recommended = 1.40 km, which is over the 1.0 km tolerance. The GPS trace's 9.652 km is not used to re-measure the distance, because POL-RD-22 says the trace shows which route was taken and when, not how long it was.\n\nTraffic justification (POL-RD-23). The LTA advisory EV-ADVIS-05003-01 is an ACCIDENT — an admissible incident type on the clause's own mapping — on the Central Expressway (CTE) towards SLE after the Balestier Road exit, valid 18:37:44 to 19:49:44, overlapping the trip window of 18:49:44 to 19:12:44. The recommended route in EV-TRIP-05003 runs via the CTE and leaves it at Braddell Road; the Balestier Road exit lies on that same CTE stretch south of Braddell, so the advisory intersects the recommended route, and the driver's contemporaneous in-app message of the same accident and jam corroborates it. The rider's argument that the advisory's null intersects_route field leaves this open is answered by the clause itself: the record hands the location question to this ruling, and the advisory's road, direction and location resolve it. The rider is right that a driver's bare assertion is not traffic data, but it does not need to be — the independent advisory carries the fact.\n\nNotice and agreement (POL-RD-21). The driver messaged at 18:57:46 ('CTE got accident after Balestier... I exit Bendemeer and go Upper Serangoon ok?'), delivered 18:57:48 and read 18:58:03, and the platform's route_deviation_detected event is at 19:00:36 — the notice was before the point of deviation. The rider replied 'Ok sure, thanks for telling' at 18:58:26, and the chat log confirms the acknowledgment, so the deviation is agreed.\n\nResult (POL-RD-2